.TH SHARKY 1 "2026-10-31" "sharky 3.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sharky \- Unix-first interactive shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sharky
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B sharky
\fB\-c\fR \fICOMMAND\fR
.br
.B sharky
\fIFILE\fR [\fIARG\fR...]
.br
.B sharky
.B state
\fB\-\-json\fR [\fB\-\-env\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sharky
is an interactive POSIX-like shell written in Rust. Commands run as real Unix
processes; the parser, executor, line editor, prompt, history and completion are
owned by the shell itself.
.PP
With no arguments and a terminal on standard input, sharky starts interactively.
With no arguments and no terminal it reads commands from standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c \fICOMMAND\fR
Run \fICOMMAND\fR and exit with its status.
.TP
.B \-i
Force interactive mode; fails when standard input or output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-login
Read login profiles before the command or system input.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON object for \fB\-c\fR or \fBstate\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-env
Include the environment in \fBstate \-\-json\fR.
.TP
.B state
Print schema version, cwd, last status and pid as JSON (requires \fB\-\-json\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-config
Do not read the configuration files.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH LANGUAGE
The prompt, \fB\-c\fR and script files share one grammar:
.IP \(bu 2
single and double quotes, backslash escapes and \fB#\fR comments;
.IP \(bu 2
variables, positional parameters, \fB${NAME:\-default}\fR operators,
substrings, case conversion, indirect expansion and \fB$(...)\fR command
substitution;
.IP \(bu 2
indexed and associative arrays, including slices and quoted \fB"${a[@]}"\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
arithmetic expansion \fB$((...))\fR and C-style \fBfor ((...))\fR loops;
.IP \(bu 2
tilde expansion and glob patterns;
.IP \(bu 2
pipelines, redirections (\fB>\fR, \fB>>\fR, \fB<\fR, \fB2>&1\fR), \fB;\fR,
\fB&&\fR and \fB||\fR lists, process substitution (\fB<(list)\fR,
\fB>(list)\fR), \fBexec\fR and \fBtime\fR;
.IP \(bu 2
\fBif\fR, \fBfor\fR, \fBwhile\fR, \fBuntil\fR, \fBcase\fR (including
\fB;;&\fR and \fB;&\fR), shell functions, groups, subshells, extglob patterns
and \fB!\fR.
.PP
This is a subset of Bash, not a replacement. Unsupported syntax fails with
status 2 instead of falling back to another shell.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration lives in \fB$SHARKY_CONFIG\fR, or
\fB${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:\-~/.config}/sharky\fR. Two files are read, each a small
declarative language with one statement per line, \fB//\fR comments and
double-quoted values:
.PP
.nf
    tide greeting off
    tide history 10000
    pearl EDITOR "nvim"
    current "~/.local/bin"
    reef ll "ls -lah"
    anchor ctrl+r history_search
.fi
.PP
.SS config.sharky
.TP
.B tide \fIkey value\fR
Shell preferences: \fBgreeting\fR, \fBautosuggest\fR, \fBhighlight\fR,
\fBstarship\fR and \fBprofile_paths\fR (\fBon\fR or \fBoff\fR) and
\fBhistory\fR (a number).
.TP
.B pearl \fINAME value\fR
Set an environment variable.
.TP
.B current \fIdirectory\fR
Prepend a directory to \fBPATH\fR.
.TP
.B reef \fIname command\fR
Define an alias.
.TP
.B anchor \fIkey action\fR
Bind a key to an editor action. Key names use \fBctrl+x\fR, \fBc+x\fR,
\fBalt+x\fR or \fBm+x\fR, named keys such as \fBtab\fR, \fBshift-tab\fR,
\fBhome\fR and \fBend\fR, or a single character. Actions include
\fBaccept_line\fR, \fBabort\fR, \fBbackward_char\fR, \fBforward_char\fR,
\fBbackward_word\fR, \fBforward_word\fR, \fBkill_line\fR, \fByank\fR,
\fBundo\fR, \fBhistory_search\fR, \fBcomplete\fR,
\fBaccept_suggestion\fR and \fBfuzzy_files\fR.
.SS theme.sharky
.TP
.B coral \fIname #RRGGBB\fR
Define a colour.
.TP
.B wave \fIkey value\fR
Set the prompt template (\fBprompt\fR) and styles. Styles reference colours with
\fB@name\fR, for example \fBwave prompt.cwd @kelp\fR.
.PP
Unknown instructions, wrong arity, invalid colours and undefined colour
references are reported as \fBfile:line\fR errors; the shell keeps running with
its defaults.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sharky/config.sharky
Shell preferences, environment, PATH entries, aliases and key bindings.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sharky/theme.sharky
Colour palette and prompt styles.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/sharky/history
Command history, limited by \fBtide history\fR.
.TP
.I /etc/sharky/profile.sharky, ~/.config/sharky/profile.sharky
Login profiles run by \fB\-l\fR.
.TP
.I /etc/profile, ~/.profile, ~/.bash_profile, ~/.zshprofile
Login mode imports only literal \fINAME=value\fR and
\fBexport \fINAME=value\fR lines from these files.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHARKY_CONFIG
Configuration directory, overriding the default.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Used to find the configuration directory.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Used to find the history file.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disables colour in the prompt when set.
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for commands, extended by \fBcurrent\fR entries.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The status of the last command, or the argument of \fBexit\fR. A syntax error
exits with 2; a command that cannot be found exits with 127; a command that
cannot be executed exits with 126.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
    sharky -c 'printf "a b\\n" | wc -w'
    sharky script.sh argument
    sharky -l -c 'echo "$LOGIN_VAR"'
    sharky --json -c 'printf hi'
    sharky state --json --env
    sharky
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bash (1),
.BR fish (1)
